<section class="about-intro"><p class="eyebrow">INDIVIDUAL STORIES · SHARED ACCOUNTABILITY</p><h1>About Commons</h1><p>Commons connects people's experiences with the records that support them. Shared explanations let someone else build on what has already been learned, and independent case records can make recurring obstacles easier to examine.</p><p><a href="index.html">Start at Commons home →</a> · <a href="entry/shared-accountability.html">Read the purpose argument →</a></p></section>
<section id="shared-records" class="about-purpose"><h2>Why these pieces belong together</h2><p>Getting a meaningful answer can require repeated letters, referrals, portal submissions, and professional help. The effort of seeking review belongs in the record alongside the disputed issue. Reducing public oversight leaves that need unresolved; Commons aims to support documentation and collective scrutiny outside dependence on a single regulator.</p><p>AI can help organize sources, transcribe recordings, assemble a chronology, and compare experiences. People check the resulting account. Content identifiers and blockchain references help identify selected versions for independent checking. Private originals remain with the case; reviewed public editions carry the shared explanation.</p><p>The current reader provides connected articles, company profiles, local contributions, and publication tools. The <a href="entry/audio-records.html">audio workflow</a> adds a design for original recordings, checked transcripts, and public derivatives. An audio intake/editor and automatic comparison of cases remain proposed work.</p><p><a href="entry/shared-accountability.html">Shared records and collective accountability →</a> · <a href="entry/audio-records.html">A conversation as a source record →</a></p></section>
<section id="stream" class="reading-tools"><h2>The collection and the stream</h2><p>The front page begins with this repository's published documents and contributions saved in your browser. Connections open one step at a time. The first portal does not fetch a live blockchain stream.</p><p><a href="index.html#view=companies">Company profiles</a> form a separate collection of legal identities, business roles, and selected official records. Articles develop reusable arguments. A case can reference both, while supplying its own evidence of who did what. Each profile includes a company-reference download.</p><p>For Litecoin, the Commons meeting point uses the transport role (the B form), mapped here to <code>LMx</code>: <code>LMxCoMMoNSzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzZU8MFW</code>. The Litecoin encoding differs from a literal B prefix. An address is a place to look; publication is open to anyone who uses it.</p><p>Adding a response makes a new record pointing to an exact parent edition. Choosing the Commons index includes its transport tag in the prepared announcement. Untagged branches can still exist and be followed explicitly; the main view does not expand every branch automatically.</p><p>Contributions are saved locally until you publish them. A publication can preserve the content on IPFS and announce its reference through Chisel. Preparing or pinning a record does not broadcast a transaction, and a hash or signature does not establish that its claims are true.</p><p><a href="publish.html#pinata">Pinata / public IPFS</a> · <a href="publish.html#announce">Litecoin announcement tools</a> · <a href="docs/PORTAL.md">Portal format and implementation notes</a></p></section>
<section class="reading-tools"><h2>Choose a reading path</h2><div class="reading-groups"><div><h3>Understand the project</h3><ul><li><a href="reading/shared-records/index.html">Shared records and the audio workflow</a></li><li><a href="reading/overview/index.html">Goals and structure</a></li><li><a href="reading/commentary/index.html">Who controls the objection?</a></li></ul></div><div><h3>Prepare a record</h3><ul><li><a href="entry/dispute-without-lawyer.html">Start with your sources</a></li><li><a href="reading/assistance/index.html">Preparation and assistance</a></li><li><a href="tools/artifacts/index.html">PDF and correspondence intake</a></li></ul></div><div><h3>Housing, debt, and institutions</h3><ul><li><a href="reading/arguments/index.html">The cost of challenging a claim</a></li><li><a href="reading/companies/index.html">Company profiles and commentary</a></li></ul></div><div><h3>Disability and access</h3><ul><li><a href="reading/autism/index.html">Autism, assessment, and benefits</a></li><li><a href="reading/disability/index.html">Disability benefits and the work of access</a></li><li><a href="reading/ptsd/index.html">PTSD and disability benefits</a></li></ul></div></div><p>The current HTML editions support browser printing. Earlier PDFs retain their original text and dates.</p><details><summary>Earlier PDF editions</summary><p><a href="reading/overview/Commons-Overview-and-Review.pdf">Overview and review</a> · <a href="reading/Commons-Request-and-Explanations.pdf">Request and explanations</a> · <a href="reading/assistance/Commons-Preparation-and-Assistance.pdf">Preparation and assistance</a></p></details><p><a href="https://github.com/johnrigler/commons#readme">README and project references</a></p></section>
